window.DECKS = window.DECKS || [];
// Multiple true/false (EDAIC Type A) stems built by recombining the 25-card
// topic blocks of decks 33-43. The statements are those cards; the exercise is
// the five-statement format, scored +1 per correct statement, no negative marking.
window.DECKS.push({
  id: "mtf-clinical",
  title: "MTF · Clinical",
  emoji: "🩺",
  subtitle: "Five-statement Type A stems rebuilt from the deck 33-43 topic blocks",
  colors: {
    front1: "#166534", front2: "#6d28d9",
    back1: "#DCFCE7", back2: "#EDE9FE", backText: "#052e16",
    chips: ["#1d4ed8","#0f766e","#B4690E","#6d28d9","#be123c","#166534","#0d9488","#b91c1c"]
  },
  cards: [

    {cat:"RSI", stem:"Regarding RSI:",
     opts:["A tight-fitting mask seal is not required for effective preoxygenation.",
           "The classical rapid sequence induction comprises preoxygenation, a predetermined induction dose, a rapid-onset relaxant and cricoid pressure, with no intervening ventilation.",
           "Cricoid pressure should be applied at 30 newtons before loss of consciousness.",
           "Adequate preoxygenation is best confirmed by an end-tidal oxygen fraction above 0.9.",
           "Preoxygenation works by replacing alveolar nitrogen with oxygen."],
     ans:["FALSE. Any leak entrains room air and the end-tidal oxygen never rises adequately. A beard, an edentulous face or a poorly held mask will all defeat preoxygenation.",
          "TRUE. The aim is to minimise the interval between loss of consciousness and a protected airway. Modern practice has modified almost every element of it.",
          "FALSE. About 10 N is applied in the awake patient, increased to 30 N after loss of consciousness. Applying full force to an awake patient causes discomfort, retching and may provoke vomiting.",
          "TRUE. This is a far better endpoint than a fixed time. Alternatives are three minutes of tidal breathing or eight vital capacity breaths, but end-tidal oxygen is the objective measure.",
          "TRUE. It fills the functional residual capacity with an oxygen reservoir — which is why anything that reduces FRC shortens the safe apnoea time so dramatically."]},

    {cat:"RSI", stem:"Concerning RSI:",
     opts:["Apnoeic oxygenation via nasal cannulae extends the time to desaturation during laryngoscopy.",
           "Evidence clearly demonstrates that cricoid pressure prevents aspiration.",
           "Cricoid pressure should be released if it is making laryngoscopy difficult.",
           "Rocuronium at rapid sequence dose produces a shorter duration of block than suxamethonium.",
           "Suxamethonium 1–1.5 mg/kg gives intubating conditions in 45–60 seconds."],
     ans:["TRUE. Oxygen moves down the pressure gradient into the alveoli even without ventilation. It does not prevent the rise in carbon dioxide.",
          "FALSE. The evidence is weak and contested; it remains in many guidelines on tradition and theoretical grounds. It can worsen the laryngoscopic view and impair mask ventilation and supraglottic airway placement.",
          "TRUE. A poor view with an unsecured airway is the greater danger. Reduce or release it, and remove it entirely if ventilation is impossible.",
          "FALSE. Rocuronium 1.2 mg/kg lasts 45–70 minutes, far longer than suxamethonium. The safety net is sugammadex 16 mg/kg, which reverses even a profound block within about three minutes.",
          "TRUE. With a duration of 3–5 minutes. Rocuronium 1.2 mg/kg is comparable in onset and is now widely preferred where sugammadex is available."]},

    {cat:"RSI", stem:"With respect to RSI:",
     opts:["Gentle mask ventilation during rapid sequence induction is now accepted in many circumstances.",
           "A 20–30 degree head-up position improves preoxygenation and prolongs safe apnoea time.",
           "Waiting for suxamethonium fasciculations to cease is a reliable indicator of readiness to intubate.",
           "In the obese patient the ear should be aligned with the sternal notch before laryngoscopy.",
           "Pregnancy beyond about 16–20 weeks is an indication for rapid sequence induction."],
     ans:["TRUE. The 'modified' RSI uses low-pressure ventilation, generally under 20 cmH₂O, to prevent desaturation. The absolute no-ventilation rule has largely been abandoned, particularly in critically ill and paediatric patients.",
          "TRUE. It increases FRC and reduces the weight of abdominal contents on the diaphragm. It is particularly valuable in obesity and pregnancy.",
          "FALSE. Fasciculations are an unreliable guide and may be absent altogether. Timing from injection, or a nerve stimulator, is better.",
          "TRUE. Ramping with blankets or a dedicated wedge improves both the laryngoscopic view and preoxygenation.",
          "TRUE. Progesterone reduces lower oesophageal sphincter tone and the gravid uterus raises intragastric pressure. Other indications include bowel obstruction, trauma, symptomatic reflux, a full stomach and emergency surgery."]},

    {cat:"RSI", stem:"In relation to RSI:",
     opts:["Diabetic gastroparesis is a recognised indication for rapid sequence induction.",
           "Capnography is the gold standard for confirming tracheal tube placement.",
           "A capnograph trace can be present briefly after oesophageal intubation.",
           "A nasogastric tube should always be removed before rapid sequence induction.",
           "The correct oral tube depth in an average adult male is approximately 23 cm at the teeth."],
     ans:["TRUE. Delayed gastric emptying means a prolonged fast does not guarantee an empty stomach.",
          "TRUE. A sustained waveform over at least six breaths. 'No trace equals wrong place' — the single most important rule in airway management, and the central lesson of NAP4.",
          "TRUE. Particularly after mask ventilation has insufflated the stomach, or after a fizzy drink. The trace will rapidly diminish over successive breaths — which is why six breaths, not one, is the standard.",
          "FALSE. It is usually left in place and aspirated — it decompresses the stomach and does not meaningfully prevent an effective cricoid seal. Opinions differ, but removal is not mandatory.",
          "TRUE. About 21 cm in an adult female. Always confirm with bilateral chest movement and auscultation, as a tube migrates with neck flexion and extension."]},

    {cat:"RSI", stem:"As regards RSI:",
     opts:["Tracheal tube cuff pressure should be kept below about 25–30 cmH₂O.",
           "An oropharyngeal airway is sized from the incisors to the angle of the mandible.",
           "A nasopharyngeal airway is contraindicated in suspected base of skull fracture.",
           "Desaturation after preoxygenation occurs faster in children than in adults.",
           "In cardiac arrest the absence of a capnograph trace reliably indicates oesophageal intubation."],
     ans:["TRUE. Above tracheal mucosal capillary perfusion pressure, ischaemia and later stenosis follow. Cuff pressure rises with nitrous oxide and with altitude.",
          "TRUE. A nasopharyngeal airway is sized from the nostril to the tragus. Too long an oral airway can push the epiglottis down and worsen obstruction.",
          "TRUE — at least on conventional teaching, because of the risk of intracranial placement. The actual reported risk is extremely low, and many would still use one carefully if the alternative is a lost airway.",
          "TRUE. A higher oxygen consumption of about 6 ml/kg/min and a smaller FRC relative to minute ventilation mean the reserve is consumed far more quickly. The same applies in pregnancy, obesity and critical illness.",
          "FALSE. With no cardiac output there may be no carbon dioxide delivered to the lungs regardless of tube position. Confirm by direct visualisation, a bougie, or ultrasound instead."]},

    {cat:"Difficult airway", stem:"Regarding difficult airway:",
     opts:["A poor Cormack–Lehane view always means intubation will be difficult.",
           "A Cormack–Lehane grade 3 view means only the epiglottis is visible.",
           "A Mallampati score of 3 or 4 reliably predicts difficult intubation.",
           "A thyromental distance of less than 6 cm suggests potential difficulty.",
           "Previous difficult intubation documented in the notes is among the strongest predictors of future difficulty."],
     ans:["FALSE. Grade 3a (epiglottis liftable) is often intubated readily with a bougie. The grade describes the VIEW, not the difficulty, and the two correlate imperfectly.",
          "TRUE. Grade 1 is the full glottis, grade 2 the posterior glottis or arytenoids, grade 3 epiglottis only, grade 4 neither glottis nor epiglottis.",
          "FALSE. Taken alone, all bedside tests have poor sensitivity and specificity. Mallampati has reasonable negative predictive value but a high false-positive rate, and should be combined with other assessments.",
          "TRUE. Roughly three finger-breadths. Other concerning findings are mouth opening under 3 cm, limited neck extension, a large tongue, a short thick neck and prominent incisors.",
          "TRUE. History outperforms every bedside test. Always ask, and always look for an alert card or bracelet."]},

    {cat:"Difficult airway", stem:"Concerning difficult airway:",
     opts:["Difficult mask ventilation is more dangerous than difficult laryngoscopy.",
           "Tracheal clicks and distal hold-up confirm correct bougie placement beyond doubt.",
           "A bougie should be advanced no further than about 25 cm at the teeth.",
           "A videolaryngoscope always makes tube delivery easier than direct laryngoscopy.",
           "A second-generation supraglottic airway is the preferred rescue device in the can't-intubate situation."],
     ans:["TRUE. Failure to oxygenate kills; failure to intubate alone does not. This is why mask ventilation predictors — beard, obesity, age over 55, edentulousness, snoring — matter so much.",
          "FALSE. Both are suggestive but can mislead — hold-up can occur in the oesophagus, and clicks may be absent. Only capnography after railroading confirms placement.",
          "TRUE. Deeper insertion risks airway trauma and pneumothorax, particularly with the coudé tip.",
          "FALSE. It reliably improves the VIEW, but a hyperangulated blade can make it harder to steer the tube around the corner. A rigid stylet or bougie shaped to match the blade is usually needed.",
          "TRUE. It provides a higher seal pressure and a gastric drain channel. Guidelines limit attempts to three before moving on."]},

    {cat:"Difficult airway", stem:"With respect to difficult airway:",
     opts:["Awake tracheal intubation should be considered when difficulty is anticipated and the airway cannot be safely managed asleep.",
           "The maximum recommended dose of topical lidocaine for airway anaesthesia is about 9 mg/kg of lean body weight.",
           "Sedation for awake intubation should be deep enough to abolish the patient's airway reflexes.",
           "Front-of-neck access in the can't-intubate can't-oxygenate situation is performed at the cricothyroid membrane.",
           "A narrow-bore cannula cricothyroidotomy with jet ventilation is the preferred first-line rescue in adults."],
     ans:["TRUE. It maintains spontaneous ventilation and airway tone throughout. It requires good topicalisation, judicious sedation, and a cooperative patient.",
          "TRUE. Airway mucosa absorbs rapidly, so toxicity is a genuine risk; use lean body weight and keep a running total.",
          "FALSE. The entire safety of the technique rests on preserved spontaneous ventilation and patient cooperation. Over-sedation converts an awake technique into the exact emergency it was meant to avoid.",
          "TRUE. Between the thyroid and cricoid cartilages — identified by the laryngeal handshake or, electively, by ultrasound.",
          "FALSE. NAP4 found a high failure rate for cannula techniques. The scalpel technique is first line in adults. Cannula techniques retain a role in children, where surgical access is more hazardous."]},

    {cat:"Difficult airway", stem:"In relation to difficult airway:",
     opts:["Obesity in itself is a reliable predictor of difficult laryngoscopy.",
           "The scalpel–bougie–tube technique uses a size 6.0 cuffed tracheal tube.",
           "Barotrauma is a major risk of jet ventilation through a narrow-bore cannula.",
           "Neuromuscular blockade should be avoided once a can't-intubate can't-oxygenate situation is declared.",
           "An airway exchange catheter may be left in situ after a difficult extubation to permit reintubation."],
     ans:["FALSE. Obesity predicts difficult MASK VENTILATION and rapid desaturation far more reliably than difficult laryngoscopy, which correlates better with neck circumference and obstructive sleep apnoea.",
          "TRUE. Laryngeal handshake, transverse stab, turn the blade caudally, bougie, then railroad the tube. It is a drilled motor sequence, practised in advance.",
          "TRUE. If the upper airway is obstructed, gas cannot escape and massive air trapping, surgical emphysema and pneumothorax follow. An expiratory route must be assured.",
          "FALSE. Full paralysis should be ENSURED. Laryngospasm and chest wall rigidity may be contributing, and front-of-neck access is easier in a still patient.",
          "TRUE. It should be clearly labelled, and oxygen insufflation through it carries a barotrauma risk if the upper airway is obstructed."]},

    {cat:"Difficult airway", stem:"As regards difficult airway:",
     opts:["Rheumatoid arthritis may cause difficulty through atlanto-axial instability and cricoarytenoid involvement.",
           "Three attempts at laryngoscopy by the same operator using the same technique is reasonable practice.",
           "Ultrasound can be used to identify the cricothyroid membrane before induction in a predicted difficult airway.",
           "Anticipated difficulty should prompt a documented airway plan shared with the whole team before induction.",
           "Extubation of a known difficult airway carries less risk than intubation."],
     ans:["TRUE. Other syndromic causes include ankylosing spondylitis, acromegaly, Down syndrome, Treacher Collins and Pierre Robin.",
          "FALSE. Repeated identical attempts cause oedema and bleeding and convert a difficult airway into an impossible one. Change something each time — operator, blade, position, adjunct — and limit total attempts.",
          "TRUE. Palpation is unreliable in the obese and in distorted necks, and marking the membrane in advance saves critical seconds.",
          "TRUE. Plans A to D stated aloud, equipment checked and present, and the person who will perform front-of-neck access identified in advance.",
          "FALSE. A substantial share of airway catastrophes occur at extubation and in recovery. The DAS extubation guideline exists precisely because this phase was neglected."]},

    {cat:"Airway emergency", stem:"Regarding airway emergencies:",
     opts:["A patient with acute upper airway obstruction should be laid flat for airway assessment.",
           "Inspiratory stridor localises the obstruction to the extrathoracic airway.",
           "Muscle relaxants should be given early in critical upper airway obstruction to improve intubating conditions.",
           "Stridor appears only when the adult airway is narrowed to roughly 50% of its normal diameter.",
           "Inhalational induction with sevoflurane maintaining spontaneous ventilation is an appropriate technique for critical upper airway obstruction."],
     ans:["FALSE. Allow them to adopt the position in which they breathe most easily, usually sitting upright. Forcing a patient flat can precipitate complete obstruction.",
          "TRUE. Extrathoracic (laryngeal or above) obstruction worsens on inspiration as negative pressure collapses the airway. Expiratory stridor suggests intrathoracic obstruction; biphasic stridor suggests a fixed lesion at or near the glottis.",
          "FALSE. Abolishing airway tone and spontaneous ventilation can convert a partial obstruction into a complete one that cannot be ventilated. Maintain spontaneous ventilation until the airway is secured.",
          "TRUE. Often less than about 5 mm. By the time stridor is audible at rest the obstruction is already critical, and silence may mean deterioration rather than improvement.",
          "TRUE. It preserves airway tone and spontaneous ventilation, and is reversible if things deteriorate. Induction should occur with the surgeon present and a tracheostomy set open."]},

    {cat:"Airway emergency", stem:"Concerning airway emergencies:",
     opts:["Heliox reduces the work of breathing in upper airway obstruction because of its low density.",
           "ACE inhibitor-induced angioedema responds reliably to adrenaline, steroids and antihistamines.",
           "Hereditary angioedema is caused by C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency.",
           "In adult supraglottitis, examination of the throat with a tongue depressor is a safe first step.",
           "Post-thyroidectomy neck haematoma causing airway compromise requires immediate opening of the wound."],
     ans:["TRUE. Flow through a narrowed upper airway is turbulent and therefore density-dependent. Its benefit is lost above about 40% oxygen, which limits use in severe hypoxaemia — it is a bridge, not a treatment.",
          "FALSE. It is bradykinin-mediated, not histamine-mediated, so it responds poorly to all three. Management is airway control and withdrawal of the drug; icatibant and C1 esterase inhibitor are used in some centres.",
          "TRUE. Treatment is C1 esterase inhibitor concentrate, icatibant or fresh frozen plasma — again, not adrenaline and steroids, which address a histamine mechanism that is not operating.",
          "FALSE. Airway instrumentation can precipitate complete obstruction, particularly in children. Minimise disturbance, give oxygen, and move to a controlled environment with senior anaesthetic and ENT presence.",
          "TRUE. Do not wait for theatre. Release the skin and deep sutures at the bedside — the obstruction is largely venous and lymphatic congestion, so decompression is the priority."]},

    {cat:"Airway emergency", stem:"With respect to airway emergencies:",
     opts:["Laryngospasm is managed with 100% oxygen, jaw thrust with continuous positive pressure, deepening of anaesthesia and then suxamethonium.",
           "Negative pressure pulmonary oedema may follow an episode of laryngospasm.",
           "Airway obstruction from a neck haematoma is caused mainly by direct tracheal compression.",
           "Oxygen should be applied to both the face and the stoma in a tracheostomy emergency.",
           "In a patient with a tracheostomy who is in respiratory distress, the first step is to remove the tracheostomy tube."],
     ans:["TRUE. Larson's point pressure behind the ear lobes may help. Suxamethonium can be given intramuscularly or intraosseously if there is no intravenous access.",
          "TRUE. Forced inspiration against a closed glottis generates markedly negative intrathoracic pressure and alveolar transudation. It usually resolves with oxygen, CPAP and time.",
          "FALSE. The dominant mechanism is venous and lymphatic obstruction causing laryngeal and pharyngeal oedema — which is why the airway may remain difficult even after the haematoma is evacuated.",
          "TRUE. Until you know whether the patient is patent upper airway or laryngectomy, both routes must be covered.",
          "FALSE. Follow the algorithm: call for help, look and listen, apply oxygen to BOTH the face and the stoma, then assess tube patency with a suction catheter. Removal comes later if the tube is proven blocked or displaced."]},

    {cat:"Airway emergency", stem:"In relation to airway emergencies:",
     opts:["A patient with an inhaled foreign body causing partial obstruction and effective coughing should receive immediate back blows.",
           "Inhalational burn injury should prompt early intubation before oedema develops.",
           "Mediastinal masses can cause complete airway collapse on induction of anaesthesia.",
           "A laryngectomy patient can be oxygenated via the face mask if the stoma is blocked.",
           "Awake tracheostomy under local anaesthesia is a legitimate option for a critically obstructed airway."],
     ans:["FALSE. If the cough is effective, encourage coughing and do nothing else. Back blows and abdominal thrusts are for INEFFECTIVE cough, and chest thrusts replace abdominal thrusts in infants and pregnancy.",
          "TRUE. Facial burns, singed nasal hair, soot in the mouth, hoarseness or stridor are the warning signs. Delay can make intubation impossible. Use an uncut tube to allow for facial swelling.",
          "TRUE. Loss of spontaneous ventilation and muscle tone removes the splinting effect. Inability to lie flat is the key warning symptom; maintain spontaneous ventilation and have rigid bronchoscopy and bypass standby available.",
          "FALSE. After laryngectomy there is NO connection between the upper airway and the trachea. All oxygenation and ventilation must be via the stoma — which is why these patients must be clearly identified at the bedside.",
          "TRUE. Particularly where the obstruction is at or above the larynx and induction is judged too dangerous. It requires a cooperative patient and an experienced surgeon."]},

    {cat:"Airway emergency", stem:"As regards airway emergencies:",
     opts:["Anaphylaxis can present with airway obstruction in the absence of any rash.",
           "Nebulised adrenaline is useful in post-extubation stridor.",
           "A supraglottic airway may buy time while definitive airway access is arranged in an emergency.",
           "Ludwig's angina is a cellulitis of the submandibular space that can obstruct the airway by displacing the tongue.",
           "Blind nasal suctioning is appropriate for suspected airway foreign body in a child."],
     ans:["TRUE. Cutaneous signs are absent in a significant minority, especially under anaesthesia where the patient is draped. Unexplained bronchospasm, hypotension or raised airway pressure should raise the suspicion.",
          "TRUE. Typically 1 mg in 5 ml. Effect is temporary, so observe for rebound; dexamethasone and heliox may be added, and reintubation must remain available.",
          "TRUE. It is a rescue device, not a destination. Guidelines limit attempts and require a clear plan for what follows.",
          "TRUE. Mouth opening is often limited, the floor of mouth is raised and indurated, and awake fibreoptic or surgical access is frequently required.",
          "FALSE. It risks pushing the object further in and provoking complete obstruction or laryngospasm. Removal should be under controlled conditions with inhalational induction and rigid bronchoscopy."]},

    {cat:"DAS guidelines", stem:"Regarding DAS guidelines:",
     opts:["If a supraglottic airway restores oxygenation, the guideline mandates proceeding with surgery.",
           "In the DAS unanticipated difficult intubation guideline, Plan A is facemask ventilation and tracheal intubation.",
           "NAP4 found that airway complications were commonest in the operating theatre rather than in ICU or the emergency department.",
           "Plan A permits a maximum of three laryngoscopy attempts, plus one further by a more experienced colleague.",
           "Plan B allows up to three attempts at supraglottic airway insertion."],
     ans:["FALSE. It explicitly requires a STOP AND THINK moment, with four options: wake the patient, proceed with the SGA, intubate via the SGA, or proceed to tracheostomy. Waking the patient is often the right answer.",
          "TRUE. Plan B is maintaining oxygenation with a supraglottic airway, Plan C is a final attempt at facemask ventilation with a view to waking the patient, and Plan D is emergency front-of-neck access.",
          "FALSE. Although most airway management occurs in theatre, events in ICU and the emergency department were far more likely to result in death or brain damage — partly through absent capnography and less experienced staff.",
          "TRUE. '3 + 1'. Each attempt should involve a change of technique, and the total is capped to prevent airway trauma.",
          "TRUE. A second-generation device is recommended, with a change of size or type between attempts."]},

    {cat:"DAS guidelines", stem:"Concerning DAS guidelines:",
     opts:["Plan D is declared when both intubation and oxygenation have failed.",
           "The DAS guideline for awake tracheal intubation recommends sedation as the primary means of achieving tolerance.",
           "NAP4 identified failure to use capnography as a contributor to deaths in intensive care.",
           "In the obstetric guideline, a declared failed intubation mandates immediate front-of-neck access.",
           "NAP4 found that poor airway assessment and failure to plan for failure were recurring themes."],
     ans:["TRUE. The can't-intubate can't-oxygenate situation. Declaring it early, out loud, is a key behavioural recommendation — delay in recognition is a recurring theme in airway deaths.",
          "FALSE. TOPICALISATION is the primary means; sedation is an adjunct and should be minimal, ideally with a single operator dedicated to it. Oxygen should be delivered throughout.",
          "TRUE. It led directly to the recommendation that continuous capnography be used for every intubated patient wherever they are cared for.",
          "FALSE. Failed intubation leads to Plan B with a supraglottic airway and a structured wake-or-proceed decision. Front-of-neck access is reserved for failure to oxygenate.",
          "TRUE. Along with repeated attempts at the same failing technique, and delayed recognition of the need for front-of-neck access."]},

    {cat:"DAS guidelines", stem:"With respect to DAS guidelines:",
     opts:["The DAS extubation guideline classifies extubation as either low-risk or at-risk.",
           "The obstetric difficult airway guideline places particular emphasis on the decision to wake the mother.",
           "A cognitive aid or wall-mounted algorithm has no demonstrated value during an airway emergency.",
           "Paediatric difficult airway guidelines separate the algorithms for difficult mask ventilation, difficult intubation and can't-intubate can't-oxygenate.",
           "Cannula cricothyroidotomy is favoured over a scalpel technique in small children."],
     ans:["TRUE. At-risk extubation prompts strategies such as delayed extubation, an airway exchange catheter, remifentanil-assisted awake extubation, or planned tracheostomy.",
          "TRUE. It provides a structured framework for whether to proceed with surgery or wake, weighing maternal and fetal condition and the feasibility of alternative anaesthesia.",
          "FALSE. Cognitive aids measurably improve adherence to correct steps under stress, which is exactly when working memory fails. Their use is explicitly encouraged.",
          "TRUE. The paediatric guidance is structured by scenario, and emphasises that desaturation is far more rapid than in adults.",
          "TRUE. The cricothyroid membrane is tiny and surgical access is correspondingly hazardous — the opposite of the adult recommendation."]},

    {cat:"DAS guidelines", stem:"In relation to DAS guidelines:",
     opts:["Guidelines recommend that the difficult airway is documented and the patient informed.",
           "Human factors and team communication are addressed explicitly in modern airway guidelines.",
           "The guidelines recommend that neuromuscular blockade is fully established before declaring failed intubation.",
           "Routine use of a bougie is discouraged because it may cause airway trauma.",
           "Guidelines recommend limiting the number of people giving instructions during an airway crisis."],
     ans:["TRUE. Including a letter to the patient and GP, an alert on the record, and consideration of a medical alert bracelet. Failure to communicate difficulty is a recurring contributor to repeat events.",
          "TRUE. Verbalising the plan, declaring failure aloud, allocating roles in advance, and using a cognitive aid are all recommended — the algorithms assume a team, not an individual.",
          "TRUE. An inadequately paralysed patient may be the reason for the poor view, and residual tone makes both supraglottic placement and front-of-neck access harder.",
          "FALSE. Early and routine bougie use improves first-pass success, and trauma is uncommon with correct depth limits. Several guidelines now encourage it as a first-line adjunct rather than a rescue.",
          "TRUE. A single clear team leader, closed-loop communication and defined roles. Multiple simultaneous instructions are a recognised cause of delay and error."]},

    {cat:"DAS guidelines", stem:"As regards DAS guidelines:",
     opts:["Second-generation supraglottic airways are preferred to first-generation devices for airway rescue.",
           "Videolaryngoscopy is recommended as the standard for all tracheal intubations in some current guidance.",
           "Every anaesthetic location should have immediate access to a difficult airway trolley with standardised contents.",
           "Front-of-neck access skills should be maintained by regular practical training.",
           "Preoxygenation is an optional step when a difficult airway is anticipated but the patient is well."],
     ans:["TRUE. The gastric drain channel and higher oropharyngeal seal pressure offer better protection and more reliable ventilation.",
          "TRUE. Increasingly so, on the basis of improved first-pass success and view. Operators are expected to be trained and practised on the device before they need it in an emergency.",
          "TRUE. Standardisation across a department means the contents are familiar under stress, and the equipment is checked and in a known place.",
          "TRUE. It is a rare, high-stakes, motor-memory skill — simulation and manikin or tissue training are explicitly recommended rather than relying on reading the algorithm.",
          "FALSE. It is more important, not less, when difficulty is expected — it buys the time that the entire algorithm depends on."]},

    {cat:"Intra-op ventilation", stem:"Regarding intra-operative ventilation:",
     opts:["Atelectasis affects a minority of patients under general anaesthesia.",
           "Intraoperative lung-protective ventilation uses tidal volumes of 6–8 ml/kg predicted body weight with PEEP.",
           "Ventilating with 100% oxygen reduces absorption atelectasis.",
           "Functional residual capacity falls by about 20% on induction of anaesthesia.",
           "Pressure-controlled ventilation delivers a decelerating inspiratory flow pattern."],
     ans:["FALSE. Some degree of atelectasis develops in the great majority of patients within minutes of induction, typically in dependent lung.",
          "TRUE. Combined with recruitment where appropriate, it reduces postoperative pulmonary complications, particularly in abdominal surgery.",
          "FALSE. High inspired oxygen INCREASES absorption atelectasis, because oxygen is taken up rapidly leaving no splinting nitrogen. A moderate FiO₂ with PEEP is preferable where oxygenation allows.",
          "TRUE. With loss of diaphragmatic tone and cephalad displacement, compounded by the supine position — the main reason atelectasis develops within minutes.",
          "TRUE. This improves distribution of gas, but tidal volume varies with compliance and resistance, so a volume alarm is essential."]},

    {cat:"Intra-op ventilation", stem:"Concerning intra-operative ventilation:",
     opts:["Pressure-regulated volume control aims to deliver a set volume at the lowest possible pressure.",
           "Volume-controlled ventilation guarantees airway pressure.",
           "Pneumoperitoneum during laparoscopy reduces compliance and raises airway pressure.",
           "A sudden rise in peak airway pressure with an unchanged plateau pressure indicates reduced compliance.",
           "Endobronchial intubation raises both peak and plateau pressures."],
     ans:["TRUE. The ventilator adjusts inspiratory pressure breath to breath using measured compliance, combining features of both basic modes.",
          "FALSE. It guarantees TIDAL VOLUME; pressure rises if compliance falls. A pressure limit must be set to prevent barotrauma.",
          "TRUE. Along with absorbed carbon dioxide raising end-tidal CO₂, and the head-down position worsening both. Tidal volume may need reducing and rate increasing.",
          "FALSE. That pattern indicates increased RESISTANCE — bronchospasm, a kinked or blocked tube, secretions. If BOTH rise together, the problem is compliance: pneumothorax, endobronchial intubation, oedema or abdominal insufflation.",
          "TRUE. The whole tidal volume enters one lung, so effective compliance halves. It also causes hypoxaemia through shunt in the unventilated lung."]},

    {cat:"Intra-op ventilation", stem:"With respect to intra-operative ventilation:",
     opts:["Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction limits shunt during one-lung ventilation.",
           "CPAP to the non-ventilated lung is a first-line measure for hypoxia during one-lung ventilation.",
           "The one-lung ventilation tidal volume should be the same as for two-lung ventilation.",
           "An upsloping phase III of the capnograph suggests heterogeneous alveolar emptying.",
           "Capnography during laparoscopy typically shows a fall in end-tidal carbon dioxide."],
     ans:["TRUE. It diverts blood from the non-ventilated lung. Volatile agents attenuate it modestly at clinical concentrations but do not abolish it.",
          "TRUE — after confirming tube position, which is the commonest cause. PEEP to the dependent lung and increased FiO₂ are the other immediate measures.",
          "FALSE. It should be reduced to about 4–6 ml/kg predicted body weight with PEEP to the ventilated lung, since the entire volume now enters one lung.",
          "TRUE. Seen in bronchospasm and COPD — the shark-fin trace.",
          "FALSE. End-tidal CO₂ RISES from absorption of insufflated carbon dioxide across the peritoneum, usually plateauing after 15–30 minutes. A sudden fall suggests gas embolism or circulatory collapse."]},

    {cat:"Intra-op ventilation", stem:"In relation to intra-operative ventilation:",
     opts:["Permissive hypercapnia is acceptable during laparoscopy in a patient with raised intracranial pressure.",
           "Positive pressure ventilation reduces venous return and can lower cardiac output.",
           "Dynamic hyperinflation is suggested by hypotension that improves on disconnecting the circuit.",
           "Prolonged inspiratory to expiratory ratio is appropriate in severe asthma.",
           "A recruitment manoeuvre followed by adequate PEEP reduces intraoperative atelectasis."],
     ans:["FALSE. Hypercapnia raises cerebral blood flow and intracranial pressure, so normocapnia must be maintained even at the cost of higher minute ventilation.",
          "TRUE. Particularly in the hypovolaemic patient, and the effect is magnified by high PEEP or dynamic hyperinflation.",
          "TRUE. Breath stacking raises intrathoracic pressure and obstructs venous return. Lengthen expiratory time and reduce rate.",
          "FALSE. Severe asthma requires a LONGER EXPIRATORY time — I:E of 1:3 or more — to allow complete exhalation and avoid gas trapping.",
          "TRUE. Though it must be balanced against haemodynamic effects, particularly in hypovolaemia."]},

    {cat:"Intra-op ventilation", stem:"As regards intra-operative ventilation:",
     opts:["Obese patients benefit from higher PEEP and reverse Trendelenburg positioning during anaesthesia.",
           "Spontaneous ventilation under anaesthesia produces better V/Q matching than controlled ventilation in the lateral position.",
           "Ventilating a patient with a bronchopleural fistula may be complicated by loss of tidal volume through the fistula.",
           "Respiratory rate should be adjusted to maintain end-tidal carbon dioxide rather than tidal volume being increased.",
           "High inspired oxygen reduces surgical site infection and should be used routinely."],
     ans:["TRUE. Both counteract the marked fall in FRC. Ventilation based on predicted rather than actual body weight is also essential.",
          "TRUE. Awake and spontaneously breathing, ventilation follows perfusion to the dependent lung. Anaesthesia and paralysis shift ventilation to the compliant upper lung while perfusion stays below.",
          "TRUE. Lung isolation, low airway pressures and sometimes spontaneous ventilation are used to limit the leak.",
          "TRUE in lung-protective practice. Tidal volume is capped for lung protection, so minute ventilation is adjusted through rate — accepting a degree of hypercapnia where safe.",
          "FALSE. The evidence is conflicting and the effect, if any, is small; set against atelectasis and oxidative stress, routine hyperoxia is not recommended."]},

    {cat:"LAST", stem:"Regarding LAST:",
     opts:["Cardiovascular signs of local anaesthetic toxicity always follow central nervous system signs.",
           "Early features of local anaesthetic systemic toxicity include perioral tingling, tinnitus and a metallic taste.",
           "Propofol is an acceptable substitute for lipid emulsion in local anaesthetic toxicity.",
           "Local anaesthetic toxicity can present up to an hour after injection.",
           "The first step in managing suspected local anaesthetic toxicity is to stop injecting and call for help."],
     ans:["FALSE. With bupivacaine, cardiovascular collapse may be the FIRST manifestation, and may occur without prodromal neurological symptoms — particularly after inadvertent intravascular injection.",
          "TRUE — followed by agitation, confusion, muscle twitching, then seizures, coma and cardiovascular collapse.",
          "FALSE. The lipid content of propofol is far too low to be useful, and the cardiovascular depression from the propofol itself is harmful.",
          "TRUE. Onset is immediate with intravascular injection but may be delayed by 30 to 60 minutes or more with large-volume tissue deposition, so monitoring must continue.",
          "TRUE — then maintain the airway, give 100% oxygen, control seizures and confirm or establish intravenous access."]},

    {cat:"LAST", stem:"Concerning LAST:",
     opts:["Lipid emulsion 20% is given as an initial bolus of 1.5 ml/kg over one minute.",
           "Lidocaine is the antiarrhythmic of choice in local anaesthetic induced arrhythmia.",
           "The maximum recommended cumulative dose of lipid emulsion is about 12 ml/kg.",
           "Lipid emulsion should be delayed until cardiac arrest occurs.",
           "Benzodiazepines are the preferred treatment for local anaesthetic induced seizures."],
     ans:["TRUE — followed by an infusion of 15 ml/kg/h, with up to two further boluses and a doubled infusion rate if the circulation does not recover.",
          "FALSE. Lidocaine is itself a local anaesthetic and must be avoided. Amiodarone is preferred; vasopressin, calcium channel blockers and beta blockers are also avoided.",
          "TRUE — in the UK guidance. The mechanism is thought to be a lipid sink plus direct cardiac metabolic effects.",
          "FALSE. It is given as soon as significant toxicity is recognised — cardiovascular instability or seizures unresponsive to initial management — not withheld until arrest.",
          "TRUE — small doses, with thiopentone or propofol in small doses as alternatives, accepting their cardiovascular depression."]},

    {cat:"LAST", stem:"With respect to LAST:",
     opts:["Cardiopulmonary resuscitation in local anaesthetic toxicity may need to continue for over an hour.",
           "Adrenaline should be given in reduced doses in local anaesthetic induced cardiac arrest.",
           "A 20 ml ampoule of 0.5% bupivacaine contains 50 mg.",
           "Ultrasound guidance reduces but does not eliminate the risk of local anaesthetic systemic toxicity.",
           "A test dose containing adrenaline producing a rise in heart rate suggests intravascular placement."],
     ans:["TRUE. Prolonged resuscitation is warranted because recovery can occur late; cardiopulmonary bypass should be considered where available.",
          "TRUE — boluses of 1 microgram/kg or less are suggested, since large doses impair resuscitation and reduce lipid emulsion efficacy in animal models.",
          "FALSE. It contains 100 mg — 0.5% is 5 mg/ml, so 20 ml is 100 mg. That already exceeds the 2 mg/kg limit for a 45 kg patient.",
          "TRUE. It reduces intravascular injection but cases still occur, so aspiration, incremental injection and vigilance remain essential.",
          "TRUE — a rise of 20 beats per minute or 15 mmHg systolic. It is unreliable in labouring women, in patients on beta blockers, and under general anaesthesia."]},

    {cat:"LAST", stem:"In relation to LAST:",
     opts:["Acidosis increases the risk of local anaesthetic toxicity.",
           "Hypoalbuminaemia increases the free fraction of local anaesthetic and so the risk of toxicity.",
           "Neonates and infants are more susceptible to local anaesthetic toxicity than adults.",
           "After successful treatment of local anaesthetic toxicity the patient can be discharged from recovery as normal.",
           "Incremental injection with intermittent aspiration reduces the risk of systemic toxicity."],
     ans:["TRUE — it increases the ionised fraction trapped intracellularly and reduces protein binding. Hypoxia, hypercapnia and hyperkalaemia all worsen cardiotoxicity.",
          "TRUE. Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein is the principal binding protein; neonates have low levels, which is one reason they are more susceptible.",
          "TRUE — reduced protein binding, immature hepatic metabolism and a larger volume of distribution. Doses are reduced accordingly.",
          "FALSE. Prolonged monitoring in a critical care area is required, because late deterioration and recurrence after lipid redistribution are described.",
          "TRUE — along with using the lowest effective dose and concentration, and avoiding injection against resistance or during patient movement."]},

    {cat:"LAST", stem:"As regards LAST:",
     opts:["Interscalene and stellate ganglion blocks carry a risk of seizure from very small volumes injected intra-arterially.",
           "Pancreatitis is a recognised complication of lipid emulsion treatment.",
           "Lipid emulsion and a treatment protocol should be immediately available wherever large-dose local anaesthetic is used.",
           "Tumescent liposuction uses doses of lidocaine far above the conventional maximum.",
           "Levobupivacaine and ropivacaine eliminate the need for toxicity precautions."],
     ans:["TRUE. Vertebral or carotid artery injection delivers drug directly to the brain, so even 1 to 2 ml can cause immediate seizure.",
          "TRUE — along with fat overload syndrome and interference with laboratory assays. Follow-up and reporting to a registry are recommended.",
          "TRUE — with the dosing regimen displayed, since the calculation is not something to improvise during an arrest.",
          "TRUE — up to 35–55 mg/kg in very dilute adrenaline-containing solution, relying on slow absorption from subcutaneous fat. Delayed toxicity many hours later is the characteristic risk.",
          "FALSE. They have a higher threshold for cardiotoxicity but are not safe in overdose — all the same precautions apply."]},

    {cat:"US-guided blocks", stem:"Regarding US-guided blocks:",
     opts:["An interscalene block reliably anaesthetises the ulnar border of the hand.",
           "Phrenic nerve palsy occurs in the majority of conventional interscalene blocks.",
           "The intercostobrachial nerve is blocked by a standard brachial plexus block.",
           "A supraclavicular block provides anaesthesia for the whole arm below the shoulder.",
           "The infraclavicular block targets the cords of the brachial plexus around the axillary artery."],
     ans:["FALSE. It spares the inferior trunk — C8 and T1 — so the ulnar distribution is unreliable. It suits shoulder rather than hand surgery.",
          "TRUE — approaching 100% with larger volumes, causing a 25% fall in lung function. It is a relative contraindication in severe respiratory disease; low volumes and more distal approaches reduce it.",
          "FALSE. It arises from T2 and is not part of the plexus, so a subcutaneous infiltration over the medial upper arm is needed for tourniquet tolerance.",
          "TRUE — the 'spinal of the arm', blocking at the level of the divisions. The corner pocket is targeted, and pneumothorax is the principal risk.",
          "TRUE — lateral, posterior and medial cords, so a U-shaped spread posterior to the artery is the aim."]},

    {cat:"US-guided blocks", stem:"Concerning US-guided blocks:",
     opts:["The axillary block requires separate blockade of the musculocutaneous nerve.",
           "A fascia iliaca block reliably blocks the sciatic nerve.",
           "A femoral nerve block provides analgesia to the anterior thigh and the knee.",
           "The transversus abdominis plane block provides visceral analgesia.",
           "The adductor canal block preserves quadriceps strength better than a femoral nerve block."],
     ans:["TRUE. It has already left the sheath and lies in coracobrachialis, so a forearm tourniquet block is incomplete without it.",
          "FALSE. It targets the femoral, obturator and lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh with variable success; the sciatic nerve is not involved.",
          "TRUE — plus the medial leg through the saphenous branch. It causes quadriceps weakness, so falls risk must be managed.",
          "FALSE. It is a somatic abdominal wall block only — visceral pain requires systemic or neuraxial analgesia.",
          "TRUE — it is predominantly sensory, blocking the saphenous nerve and the nerve to vastus medialis, and is favoured for knee arthroplasty with early mobilisation."]},

    {cat:"US-guided blocks", stem:"With respect to US-guided blocks:",
     opts:["A popliteal sciatic block anaesthetises the foot but spares the medial malleolus.",
           "A rectus sheath block is useful for midline abdominal incisions.",
           "Intraneural injection always results in permanent nerve injury.",
           "The erector spinae plane block is performed with the needle contacting the transverse process.",
           "The serratus anterior plane block is useful for rib fractures and thoracotomy."],
     ans:["TRUE. The medial aspect is supplied by the saphenous nerve from the femoral, so a saphenous or adductor canal block is added for complete foot anaesthesia.",
          "TRUE — blocking the terminal branches of T7 to T11 as they enter the posterior rectus sheath. The posterior wall must be identified to avoid peritoneal puncture.",
          "FALSE. Subepineurial, extrafascicular injection is surprisingly often without sequelae; it is INTRAfascicular injection that is damaging. Neither should be aimed for.",
          "TRUE. Injection deep to erector spinae and superficial to the transverse process; spread is craniocaudal and partly paravertebral.",
          "TRUE — blocking the lateral cutaneous branches of the intercostal nerves, superficial or deep to serratus anterior at the fourth to fifth rib."]},

    {cat:"US-guided blocks", stem:"In relation to US-guided blocks:",
     opts:["Paravertebral blockade produces unilateral somatic and sympathetic blockade.",
           "An in-plane needle approach shows the needle along its whole length.",
           "Hydrolocation is the use of a small volume of injectate to confirm needle tip position.",
           "Peripheral nerves in the distal limb appear hypoechoic while the cervical roots appear hyperechoic.",
           "Injection pressures above 15 psi suggest intrafascicular needle placement."],
     ans:["TRUE. It gives analgesia comparable to epidural for thoracotomy with less hypotension and urinary retention.",
          "TRUE — the needle is parallel to the probe's long axis. Out-of-plane shows a cross-section only, so the tip position is less certain.",
          "TRUE. A small test injection reveals the tip; if no spread is seen, the tip is not where it appears to be and injection must stop.",
          "FALSE — the reverse. Brachial plexus roots in the neck are dark, round and monofascicular (hypoechoic); peripheral nerves distally are bright and honeycombed (hyperechoic).",
          "TRUE. High opening pressure is associated with intraneural injection, which is why injection against resistance must never be forced."]},

    {cat:"US-guided blocks", stem:"As regards US-guided blocks:",
     opts:["Anisotropy means a structure's appearance changes with the angle of insonation.",
           "Stop before block is a recommended safety step before any regional technique.",
           "Wrong-site block is a recognised never event in regional anaesthesia.",
           "Performing blocks under general anaesthesia increases the risk of nerve injury because warning symptoms are lost.",
           "Compartment syndrome will always be masked by a regional block."],
     ans:["TRUE — marked in tendons and nerves, which is why tilting the probe helps distinguish a nerve from a tendon.",
          "TRUE — a dedicated pause to confirm patient, site, side, drug, dose, equipment and monitoring, in addition to the surgical checklist.",
          "TRUE — the reason for marking the side and for the stop-before-block pause, since the block is often performed away from the checklist moment.",
          "TRUE — paraesthesia and pain on injection are lost. It is accepted in children and in selected adults, with ultrasound and pressure monitoring.",
          "FALSE. Compartment syndrome may still present with breakthrough pain or increasing analgesic requirement; the concern is real but blocks are not absolutely contraindicated, provided monitoring is explicit."]},

    {cat:"Neuraxial & anticoagulants", stem:"Regarding neuraxial & anticoagulants:",
     opts:["Heparin may be restarted immediately after an epidural catheter is removed.",
           "Prophylactic low molecular weight heparin requires a 12-hour interval before neuraxial puncture.",
           "An INR below 2.0 is acceptable for neuraxial blockade in a warfarinised patient.",
           "Unfractionated heparin given subcutaneously requires a 4-hour interval before neuraxial blockade.",
           "Clopidogrel requires a 7-day interval before neuraxial blockade."],
     ans:["FALSE. An interval of about 1 hour for unfractionated heparin and 4 hours for low molecular weight heparin is recommended after removal.",
          "TRUE — 12 hours for a prophylactic dose and 24 hours for a treatment dose, with 4 hours before the next dose after the block or catheter removal.",
          "FALSE. The INR should be below 1.5 — some guidance accepts 1.4. Bridging and reversal depend on the indication and thrombotic risk.",
          "TRUE — 4 hours or a normal APTT. After intravenous heparin the interval is also 4 hours with a normal APTT.",
          "TRUE. Prasugrel is also 7 days, ticagrelor 5 days, ticlopidine 10 days. Aspirin alone requires no interval."]},

    {cat:"Neuraxial & anticoagulants", stem:"Concerning neuraxial & anticoagulants:",
     opts:["Rivaroxaban requires at least 72 hours before neuraxial blockade when given at treatment dose.",
           "Aspirin alone is a contraindication to spinal anaesthesia.",
           "Dabigatran intervals depend on creatinine clearance.",
           "Surgical decompression of a vertebral canal haematoma is best within 24 hours.",
           "Thrombolytic therapy is an absolute contraindication to neuraxial blockade."],
     ans:["TRUE in current UK guidance — 72 hours for a treatment dose and at least 18 hours for a prophylactic dose, longer if renal function is impaired.",
          "FALSE. Aspirin monotherapy and NSAIDs are not a contraindication. Risk rises when combined with another agent.",
          "TRUE — it is renally cleared, so intervals lengthen markedly in renal impairment, up to several days.",
          "FALSE. The window is much shorter — ideally within 8 hours of symptom onset. Delay beyond that markedly reduces neurological recovery.",
          "TRUE in practical terms — a 10-day interval is recommended, and the block should be avoided where possible."]},

    {cat:"Neuraxial & anticoagulants", stem:"With respect to neuraxial & anticoagulants:",
     opts:["Removal of an epidural catheter carries a similar risk of haematoma to its insertion.",
           "Vertebral canal haematoma most often presents with back pain and progressive weakness.",
           "The incidence of permanent harm from epidural blockade is greater in the obstetric than the perioperative population.",
           "A platelet count above 70 to 80 × 10⁹/l is generally considered acceptable for spinal anaesthesia in a stable patient.",
           "The motor block in spinal anaesthesia extends about two segments above the sensory level."],
     ans:["TRUE — about half of reported haematomas relate to removal, so the same timing rules apply to removal as to insertion.",
          "TRUE — with sensory loss, sphincter disturbance, and a block that is denser or longer than expected. Urgent MRI and decompression offer the best outcome.",
          "FALSE. It is LOWER in obstetrics — young, healthy, rarely anticoagulated patients. The highest risk is in perioperative patients receiving thromboprophylaxis.",
          "TRUE — with trend and cause mattering as much as the number. A falling count in pre-eclampsia is more concerning than a stable low count in ITP.",
          "FALSE. The MOTOR level is about two segments BELOW the sensory level; it is the SYMPATHETIC block that extends about two segments above it. A T10 sensory block implies sympathetic blockade to T8."]},

    {cat:"Neuraxial & anticoagulants", stem:"In relation to neuraxial & anticoagulants:",
     opts:["A single-shot spinal carries a lower haematoma risk than an epidural catheter.",
           "Spinal anaesthesia causes hypotension chiefly through blockade of sympathetic fibres from T1 to L2.",
           "The spinal cord in adults ends at L1 to L2 and the dura at S2.",
           "Epidural blood patch has a permanent success rate of around 95% at first attempt.",
           "Post-dural puncture headache is characteristically relieved by lying flat."],
     ans:["TRUE — smaller needle, no catheter, no removal event. Where a neuraxial technique is needed in a borderline patient, a spinal is the lower-risk choice.",
          "TRUE — reducing venous return and systemic vascular resistance. Cardioaccelerator fibres from T1 to T4 account for bradycardia in a high block.",
          "TRUE — so lumbar puncture below L3 avoids the cord. In neonates the cord ends at L3 and the dura at S4.",
          "FALSE. Immediate relief is common but permanent cure is around 60 to 70% at first attempt, and a second patch is sometimes needed.",
          "TRUE — postural, fronto-occipital, often with neck stiffness, photophobia and sometimes cranial nerve signs. Onset is usually within 48 hours."]},

    {cat:"Neuraxial & anticoagulants", stem:"As regards neuraxial & anticoagulants:",
     opts:["Transient neurological symptoms after spinal anaesthesia are more common with lidocaine than bupivacaine.",
           "Adding glucose to bupivacaine makes it hyperbaric so it sinks with gravity.",
           "Chlorhexidine in alcohol is the recommended skin preparation for neuraxial blockade but must be kept away from the needle and catheter.",
           "Caudal blockade in children is performed through the sacrococcygeal ligament into the sacral hiatus.",
           "Epidural abscess most commonly presents within 24 hours of insertion."],
     ans:["TRUE — transient neurological symptoms, formerly transient radicular irritation, with a relative risk several times higher for intrathecal lidocaine.",
          "TRUE — the basis of positioning to control spread. Plain solutions are slightly hypobaric relative to CSF at body temperature.",
          "TRUE — it is neurotoxic. It should be sprayed rather than poured, and allowed to dry fully.",
          "TRUE — between the sacral cornua. The dura ends lower in infants, so needle depth must be limited and dosing follows volume-based regimens.",
          "FALSE. Abscess usually presents days later, with back pain, fever and raised inflammatory markers, which is why catheter sites require daily review and clear follow-up after discharge."]},

    {cat:"Vascular", stem:"Regarding vascular surgery:",
     opts:["Elective abdominal aortic aneurysm repair is generally offered once the diameter exceeds 4.5 cm in men.",
           "Patients presenting for vascular surgery have a high prevalence of coexisting coronary artery disease.",
           "Unclamping the aorta characteristically causes hypertension.",
           "Open abdominal aortic aneurysm repair carries a higher perioperative mortality than endovascular repair.",
           "Aortic cross-clamping increases afterload and may precipitate left ventricular failure."],
     ans:["FALSE. The usual threshold is above 5.5 cm — or rapid expansion, or symptoms. Below that, surveillance outperforms early repair.",
          "TRUE. Atherosclerosis is generalised, so around half have significant coronary disease and a third have diabetes — the perioperative cardiac event rate is among the highest of any surgical group.",
          "FALSE. Unclamping causes HYPOTENSION — sudden reduction in resistance plus washout of acid and vasoactive metabolites from ischaemic tissue. Volume loading and gradual release anticipate it.",
          "TRUE — early mortality favours EVAR. The long-term survival advantage disappears by a few years, with more reintervention and surveillance after EVAR.",
          "TRUE. Systemic vascular resistance and filling pressures rise; vasodilators and deepening anaesthesia are used in anticipation."]},

    {cat:"Vascular", stem:"Concerning vascular surgery:",
     opts:["Suprarenal cross-clamping carries a higher risk of renal failure than infrarenal clamping.",
           "Spinal cord protection in thoracoabdominal repair is achieved by RAISING cerebrospinal fluid pressure.",
           "Spinal cord ischaemia after thoracoabdominal aortic surgery relates to the artery of Adamkiewicz.",
           "Carotid endarterectomy under local anaesthesia has been shown to improve outcome over general anaesthesia.",
           "Cerebral monitoring options during carotid endarterectomy include stump pressure, transcranial Doppler and near-infrared spectroscopy."],
     ans:["TRUE — direct renal ischaemia. Even infrarenal clamping reduces renal blood flow, so acute kidney injury remains common.",
          "FALSE — CSF pressure is LOWERED by drainage. Spinal cord perfusion pressure is arterial pressure minus CSF pressure, so draining CSF improves perfusion.",
          "TRUE — the dominant anterior spinal supply to the lower cord, usually arising between T9 and T12. Paraplegia is the feared complication.",
          "FALSE. The GALA trial found no significant difference in stroke, myocardial infarction or death. Local anaesthesia permits awake neurological monitoring, which is a practical rather than proven advantage.",
          "TRUE — along with EEG and, in the awake patient, direct neurological assessment, which remains the gold standard."]},

    {cat:"Vascular", stem:"With respect to vascular surgery:",
     opts:["A superficial and deep cervical plexus block provides anaesthesia for awake carotid endarterectomy.",
           "Blood pressure lability is characteristic after carotid endarterectomy.",
           "Neck haematoma after carotid surgery is primarily a bleeding rather than an airway problem.",
           "Cerebral hyperperfusion syndrome after carotid surgery presents with headache, seizures and focal deficit.",
           "Beta blockers should be started on the day of surgery in all vascular patients to reduce cardiac risk."],
     ans:["TRUE — blocking C2 to C4. Deep block risks phrenic palsy, vertebral artery injection and recurrent laryngeal nerve block, so many now use superficial or intermediate blocks only.",
          "TRUE — from baroreceptor denervation and manipulation. Both hypertension, risking hyperperfusion syndrome and haemorrhage, and hypotension are common.",
          "FALSE. It is an AIRWAY emergency — venous congestion and laryngeal oedema make intubation difficult, so the wound may need opening at the bedside before any attempt.",
          "TRUE — in a chronically hypoperfused territory with lost autoregulation. Strict blood pressure control is the treatment.",
          "FALSE. Acute high-dose initiation increased stroke and mortality in the POISE trial. Established beta blockade is continued; new treatment is begun well in advance, if at all."]},

    {cat:"Vascular", stem:"In relation to vascular surgery:",
     opts:["Temperature maintenance is less important in vascular surgery than in other major surgery.",
           "Statins should be continued through the perioperative period in vascular patients.",
           "Aspirin is usually continued through vascular surgery.",
           "Heparin is reversed with protamine at a dose of 1 mg per 1000 units of heparin.",
           "Permissive hypotension is the strategy of choice in ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm before clamping."],
     ans:["FALSE. Large exposed cavities and long procedures make hypothermia likely, and it worsens coagulopathy, cardiac events and wound infection — active warming is essential.",
          "TRUE. Withdrawal is associated with worse cardiac outcomes, and there is evidence of benefit from continuation and even initiation.",
          "TRUE. The thrombotic risk of stopping it generally outweighs the bleeding risk in this population.",
          "FALSE. The ratio is about 1 mg per 100 units — ten times more protamine than that figure suggests. Protamine itself causes hypotension, pulmonary hypertension and anaphylactoid reactions, so it is given slowly.",
          "TRUE. Aggressive resuscitation dislodges clot and worsens bleeding; the aim is a palpable pulse or conscious patient until proximal control is achieved."]},

    {cat:"Vascular", stem:"As regards vascular surgery:",
     opts:["Induction of anaesthesia for ruptured aneurysm should be delayed until the surgeon is scrubbed and ready.",
           "Revascularisation before non-cardiac surgery reduces perioperative cardiac events in stable coronary disease.",
           "Regional anaesthesia is often preferred for lower limb amputation and fistula surgery.",
           "Endovascular aneurysm repair can be performed under regional or local anaesthesia.",
           "Preoperative regional blockade prevents phantom limb pain after amputation."],
     ans:["TRUE. Loss of abdominal wall tone and sympathetic tone on induction can precipitate catastrophic haemorrhage, so induction happens in theatre with the team prepared.",
          "FALSE. The CARP trial showed no benefit from prophylactic coronary revascularisation before vascular surgery in stable disease — and it delays the operation.",
          "TRUE — avoiding general anaesthesia in a frail comorbid population, with good postoperative analgesia and avoidance of airway instrumentation.",
          "TRUE — one of its attractions in high-risk patients, though conversion to general anaesthesia must always be possible.",
          "FALSE. The evidence does not support prevention, although it provides excellent perioperative analgesia. Established phantom pain is managed with neuropathic agents and mirror therapy."]},

    {cat:"ENT & ophthalmic", stem:"Regarding ENT and ophthalmic anaesthesia:",
     opts:["In the event of an airway fire the first action is to flood the field with saline.",
           "A shared airway is the defining anaesthetic challenge of ENT surgery.",
           "Airway fire risk is increased by a high inspired oxygen concentration and the use of nitrous oxide.",
           "Post-tonsillectomy bleeding patients are hypovolaemic with a full stomach of swallowed blood.",
           "Children with obstructive sleep apnoea require reduced opioid doses after tonsillectomy."],
     ans:["FALSE. The first action is to STOP the gas flow and remove the burning tube; then flood with saline, ventilate with air, and re-examine the airway by bronchoscopy.",
          "TRUE. Access, soiling with blood, the risk of airway fire, and the need for a smooth emergence without coughing all follow from it.",
          "TRUE. Both support combustion. Laser surgery requires FiO₂ below about 0.3, no nitrous oxide, a laser-resistant tube and saline-filled cuffs.",
          "TRUE — plus a potentially difficult airway from blood and oedema, and often a frightened child. Resuscitation, two suckers and a skilled assistant are essential before induction.",
          "TRUE. They are abnormally sensitive to opioid respiratory depression; codeine is contraindicated in children after tonsillectomy because of ultra-rapid metaboliser deaths."]},

    {cat:"ENT & ophthalmic", stem:"Concerning ENT and ophthalmic anaesthesia:",
     opts:["Dexamethasone reduces postoperative nausea and pain after tonsillectomy.",
           "The afferent limb of the oculocardiac reflex is the vagus and the efferent limb the trigeminal nerve.",
           "A throat pack must be formally recorded and its removal checked.",
           "Middle ear surgery is one of the highest-risk operations for postoperative nausea and vomiting.",
           "Nitrous oxide is contraindicated during tympanoplasty."],
     ans:["TRUE — and reduces oedema. Concern about increased bleeding has not been borne out at usual doses.",
          "FALSE — the reverse. Afferent is TRIGEMINAL, efferent is VAGAL. It is commonest during squint surgery in children; treatment is to stop traction, then give an antimuscarinic.",
          "TRUE. Retained throat packs are a never event, requiring a documented two-person check and a visible label or marker.",
          "TRUE — vestibular stimulation. Multimodal prophylaxis with two or more antiemetics is standard, and nitrous oxide is avoided.",
          "TRUE. It diffuses into the middle ear and raises pressure, which can displace a tympanic graft; it also causes negative pressure on withdrawal."]},

    {cat:"ENT & ophthalmic", stem:"With respect to ENT and ophthalmic anaesthesia:",
     opts:["Recurrent laryngeal nerve monitoring during thyroid surgery requires avoidance of long-acting neuromuscular blockade.",
           "Tracheomalacia and recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy are recognised causes of airway obstruction after thyroidectomy.",
           "A peribulbar block carries a higher risk of globe perforation than a retrobulbar block.",
           "A retrosternal goitre causing tracheal compression is a reason for inhalational induction with maintained spontaneous ventilation.",
           "High-flow nasal oxygen can be used for apnoeic oxygenation during tubeless laryngeal surgery."],
     ans:["TRUE. Either no relaxant, or a short-acting agent with reversal, so that nerve stimulation produces a detectable response.",
          "TRUE — along with haematoma and hypocalcaemia from parathyroid injury, which typically appears at 24 to 72 hours.",
          "FALSE — the reverse. Peribulbar injection outside the muscle cone is SAFER; retrobulbar block carries higher risks of perforation, optic nerve injury and brainstem anaesthesia.",
          "TRUE — or awake fibreoptic intubation. Muscle relaxation can cause complete obstruction of a dynamically compressed airway.",
          "TRUE — the THRIVE technique, which extends safe apnoea time considerably. Carbon dioxide still rises, so duration is limited."]},

    {cat:"ENT & ophthalmic", stem:"In relation to ENT and ophthalmic anaesthesia:",
     opts:["Intraocular pressure normally lies between 10 and 20 mmHg.",
           "Suxamethonium raises intraocular pressure and is best avoided in an open eye injury.",
           "Nitrous oxide must be avoided in the presence of an intraocular gas bubble.",
           "A sub-Tenon block requires a sharp needle.",
           "Brainstem anaesthesia is a recognised complication of retrobulbar block."],
     ans:["TRUE. It is raised by coughing, straining, suxamethonium, hypercapnia and external pressure, and lowered by hypocapnia and most anaesthetic agents.",
          "TRUE — a rise of about 10 mmHg for several minutes. In practice the risk of aspiration may still favour it, and rocuronium with sugammadex is the usual alternative.",
          "TRUE — it expands sulphur hexafluoride and similar bubbles, raising intraocular pressure and risking retinal ischaemia. It is avoided for weeks after such surgery.",
          "FALSE. It uses a blunt cannula through a small conjunctival incision, which is why it has become popular as a lower-risk alternative to needle blocks.",
          "TRUE — from injection into the optic nerve sheath, causing apnoea, convulsions and cardiovascular collapse within minutes. Resuscitation facilities must be available."]},

    {cat:"ENT & ophthalmic", stem:"As regards ENT and ophthalmic anaesthesia:",
     opts:["Topical anaesthesia is adequate for most routine phacoemulsification cataract surgery.",
           "Timolol eye drops can cause bradycardia and bronchospasm.",
           "Acetazolamide causes a metabolic acidosis with hypokalaemia.",
           "Coughing on emergence is particularly undesirable after both intraocular and middle ear surgery.",
           "Stridor at rest in a child indicates airway narrowing to a critical degree."],
     ans:["TRUE — with intracameral local anaesthetic. It avoids needle risks entirely and suits a largely elderly day-case population.",
          "TRUE — systemic absorption of a non-selective beta blocker. Phenylephrine and acetazolamide drops have their own systemic effects.",
          "TRUE — carbonic anhydrase inhibition producing a hyperchloraemic acidosis and bicarbonate diuresis, relevant in patients on long-term treatment for glaucoma.",
          "TRUE — it raises intraocular and venous pressure and can disrupt grafts. Deep extubation, remifentanil, lidocaine and a supraglottic airway are strategies used.",
          "TRUE — resistance rises with the fourth power of radius, so stridor at rest signifies advanced narrowing. Agitation and instrumentation must be avoided until the airway is secured in theatre."]},

    {cat:"Day-case", stem:"Regarding day-case anaesthesia:",
     opts:["A high body mass index alone is a contraindication to day-case surgery.",
           "Day-case surgery should be the default pathway for elective surgery where possible.",
           "Obstructive sleep apnoea is an absolute contraindication to day-case surgery.",
           "Suitability for day surgery depends more on functional status and social circumstances than on ASA grade.",
           "Patients should have a responsible adult to accompany them home and stay overnight."],
     ans:["FALSE. Obesity is not itself a bar; what matters is comorbidity, obstructive sleep apnoea severity, airway assessment and the facilities available.",
          "TRUE — the current expectation in most health systems, with inpatient admission requiring justification rather than the reverse.",
          "FALSE. Well-controlled OSA on CPAP, with careful selection of procedure and avoidance of long-acting opioids, is commonly managed as a day case.",
          "TRUE. A stable ASA 3 patient with a responsible escort and a telephone is often a better candidate than an unsupported ASA 2.",
          "TRUE — along with reasonable access to a telephone and transport, and an understanding of the discharge instructions."]},

    {cat:"Day-case", stem:"Concerning day-case anaesthesia:",
     opts:["Patients should be told not to drive for 24 hours after general anaesthesia.",
           "An ex-premature infant can be discharged as a day case once past 44 weeks post-conceptual age.",
           "Pain and nausea are the commonest reasons for unplanned admission after day surgery.",
           "Long-acting peripheral nerve blocks preclude day-case discharge.",
           "Multimodal analgesia with local anaesthetic infiltration reduces opioid requirement after day surgery."],
     ans:["TRUE — along with avoiding alcohol, important decisions and operating machinery. Some procedures and drugs require longer.",
          "FALSE. The risk of postoperative apnoea persists to around 60 weeks post-conceptual age, so overnight monitoring is required until then. Children under one are generally not suitable for a stand-alone unit at all.",
          "TRUE — along with surgical complications, bleeding, urinary retention and drowsiness. Both are largely preventable.",
          "FALSE. Discharge with a working block is routine, provided the patient understands limb protection, has oral analgesia for when the block wears off, and has clear contact arrangements.",
          "TRUE — paracetamol, NSAIDs where permitted, and wound infiltration or a regional block, so that opioids become rescue rather than routine."]},

    {cat:"Day-case", stem:"With respect to day-case anaesthesia:",
     opts:["Propofol-based anaesthesia reduces postoperative nausea compared with volatile anaesthesia.",
           "The Apfel score uses female sex, non-smoking status, previous nausea or motion sickness and postoperative opioid use.",
           "Routine mandatory oral intake and voiding are required before discharge from a day unit.",
           "Modified Aldrete or similar scoring systems are used to assess readiness for discharge.",
           "Clear fluids must be stopped six hours before day surgery, as for solids."],
     ans:["TRUE — one reason total intravenous anaesthesia suits day surgery, along with avoidance of nitrous oxide and prophylactic antiemetics in those at risk.",
          "TRUE. Each factor adds roughly 20% to the baseline risk, guiding how many prophylactic antiemetics to give.",
          "FALSE. Both have been abandoned as universal criteria — insisting on them delays discharge without benefit. Voiding is required after procedures with a specific retention risk.",
          "TRUE — with the modern emphasis on fast-tracking, where patients meeting criteria in theatre may bypass the first-stage recovery area.",
          "FALSE. Clear fluids are allowed until two hours before, and encouraging them reduces thirst, nausea and discomfort. Six hours applies to solids."]},

    {cat:"Day-case", stem:"In relation to day-case anaesthesia:",
     opts:["Supraglottic airway devices are well suited to day-case anaesthesia.",
           "Urinary retention after day-case spinal anaesthesia is more likely with long-acting agents and with inguinal hernia repair.",
           "Dexamethasone used as an antiemetic causes clinically significant hyperglycaemia in diabetic day-case patients.",
           "Spinal anaesthesia is unsuitable for day surgery because of delayed discharge.",
           "Diabetic patients should be placed first on the day-case list where possible."],
     ans:["TRUE. They avoid relaxants, reduce sore throat and coughing, and permit a smoother and faster emergence than tracheal intubation.",
          "TRUE — along with male sex, older age and anticholinergic drugs. It is a recognised cause of unplanned admission.",
          "TRUE — a transient rise that matters in poorly controlled diabetes, so glucose should be monitored. The antiemetic benefit usually still favours its use.",
          "FALSE. With short-acting agents such as prilocaine or chloroprocaine and low-dose techniques, day-case spinal anaesthesia is well established — bupivacaine is the agent that delays discharge.",
          "TRUE — minimising fasting, allowing resumption of normal diet and medication, and reducing the need for variable-rate insulin infusion."]},

    {cat:"Day-case", stem:"As regards day-case anaesthesia:",
     opts:["Written as well as verbal discharge information should be given, including who to contact.",
           "Nurse-led discharge against agreed criteria is an accepted model in day surgery.",
           "Preoperative assessment should ideally be nurse-led and protocol-driven with anaesthetic review of exceptions.",
           "Patients taking regular opioids are usually poor candidates for day surgery.",
           "Day surgery carries a higher rate of serious complications than inpatient surgery for the same procedure."],
     ans:["TRUE. Recall of verbal instruction after anaesthesia is poor, so written instructions and a contact number are a minimum standard.",
          "TRUE — with medical review reserved for patients failing the criteria. It is one of the main drivers of day-unit efficiency.",
          "TRUE — identifying problems early enough to optimise rather than cancel, which is the main determinant of a unit's cancellation rate.",
          "TRUE — in practice their analgesic requirement is harder to meet within a day-case pathway, and they need planned multimodal regimens and sometimes admission.",
          "FALSE. Outcomes are at least as good and often better, with less infection, less thromboembolism and fewer cancellations, in a selected population."]},

    {cat:"Anaemia & blood management", stem:"Regarding anaemia & blood management:",
     opts:["The World Health Organization defines anaemia as haemoglobin below 120 g/l in men and 110 g/l in non-pregnant women.",
           "Preoperative anaemia is an independent predictor of perioperative morbidity and mortality.",
           "Ferritin is a reliable marker of iron stores in the presence of inflammation.",
           "Anaemia should ideally be identified and treated at least four weeks before major elective surgery.",
           "The three pillars of patient blood management are optimising red cell mass, minimising blood loss, and optimising tolerance of anaemia."],
     ans:["FALSE. The thresholds are 130 g/l in men and 120 g/l in non-pregnant women; 110 g/l is the figure for pregnancy. Patient blood management guidance increasingly uses 130 g/l for both sexes preoperatively.",
          "TRUE — even mild anaemia, and independently of transfusion. It is why anaemia is treated rather than simply transfused on the day.",
          "FALSE. Ferritin is an acute phase protein and rises with inflammation, masking deficiency — which is why transferrin saturation and CRP are measured alongside it.",
          "TRUE. Iron, with erythropoietin where indicated, needs time to work, so referral at the point of listing rather than at pre-assessment is the goal.",
          "TRUE. Transfusion is the fallback, not the strategy."]},

    {cat:"Anaemia & blood management", stem:"Concerning anaemia & blood management:",
     opts:["A ferritin below 30 micrograms/l indicates absolute iron deficiency.",
           "Liberal transfusion improves outcomes in critically ill patients.",
           "Intravenous iron corrects anaemia faster than oral iron.",
           "ABO-incompatible transfusion causes a delayed extravascular haemolytic reaction.",
           "Oral iron is best absorbed when taken with vitamin C and on an empty stomach."],
     ans:["TRUE. Between 30 and 100, with a transferrin saturation below 20% or raised inflammatory markers, suggests functional iron deficiency.",
          "FALSE. The TRICC trial and subsequent studies showed restrictive strategies to be equivalent or superior, and transfusion carries dose-dependent risk.",
          "TRUE — days to weeks rather than months, with better tolerance and no dependence on gut absorption. It is preferred when surgery is close or oral iron has failed.",
          "FALSE. ABO incompatibility causes ACUTE INTRAVASCULAR haemolysis, with fever, loin pain, hypotension, haemoglobinuria and DIC. Delayed extravascular haemolysis follows other red cell antibodies.",
          "TRUE — and alternate-day dosing may be better absorbed than daily dosing, because hepcidin rises after a dose."]},

    {cat:"Anaemia & blood management", stem:"With respect to anaemia & blood management:",
     opts:["A restrictive transfusion threshold of 70 g/l is appropriate for most stable patients.",
           "Single-unit transfusion with reassessment is the recommended practice in stable non-bleeding patients.",
           "Washed cell-salvaged blood contains platelets and clotting factors as well as red cells.",
           "Transfusion-associated circulatory overload is now a leading cause of transfusion-related death.",
           "TRALI presents within six hours of transfusion with hypoxia and bilateral infiltrates."],
     ans:["TRUE — with 80 g/l used in acute coronary syndrome, cardiac surgery and some oncology settings. Thresholds are a prompt to assess, not an automatic trigger.",
          "TRUE. 'One unit, then reassess' — the old habit of prescribing two units together is specifically discouraged.",
          "FALSE. Washing removes them, so large-volume salvage still requires separate replacement of platelets, fibrinogen and factors. Salvage does reduce allogeneic red cell use in cardiac, vascular, orthopaedic and obstetric surgery.",
          "TRUE — and largely preventable by careful assessment, single units and slower rates in at-risk patients.",
          "TRUE — and is distinguished from circulatory overload by the absence of raised filling pressures and the lack of response to diuretics."]},

    {cat:"Anaemia & blood management", stem:"In relation to anaemia & blood management:",
     opts:["Bacterial contamination risk is greater for platelets than for red cells.",
           "Tranexamic acid reduces blood loss in a wide range of surgery.",
           "Acute normovolaemic haemodilution involves removing blood and replacing it with crystalloid or colloid.",
           "Cell salvage is absolutely contraindicated in obstetric haemorrhage.",
           "Fibrinogen is typically the first coagulation factor to fall to a critical level in major haemorrhage."],
     ans:["TRUE — platelets are stored at room temperature. It is now a greater infectious hazard than viral transmission in most developed transfusion services.",
          "TRUE — in joint arthroplasty, cardiac, obstetric and trauma surgery, with no clear increase in thrombotic events at usual doses.",
          "TRUE. Surgical blood is then more dilute, and the patient's own blood is returned afterwards. It is used selectively, mainly where allogeneic blood is unavailable or refused.",
          "FALSE. It is now accepted in obstetrics, with leucodepletion filters; the historical concern about amniotic fluid embolism has not been borne out.",
          "TRUE — below about 1.5 to 2 g/l. It is replaced with cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate."]},

    {cat:"Anaemia & blood management", stem:"As regards anaemia & blood management:",
     opts:["Prothrombin complex concentrate is the preferred reversal agent for warfarin in major bleeding.",
           "Idarucizumab reverses dabigatran and andexanet alfa reverses the factor Xa inhibitors.",
           "Irradiated blood components are required for patients at risk of transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease.",
           "Group O negative red cells can be given to any recipient in an emergency.",
           "Jehovah's Witness patients universally refuse all blood fractions including albumin and clotting factor concentrates."],
     ans:["TRUE — with vitamin K, because it works within minutes and avoids the volume load of plasma.",
          "TRUE. Where unavailable, prothrombin complex concentrate is used for Xa inhibitors, and dabigatran is dialysable.",
          "TRUE — including those who have had purine analogues, intrauterine transfusion, or granulocyte transfusions, and patients with some congenital immunodeficiencies.",
          "TRUE — the universal red cell donor. For plasma it is the reverse: AB plasma is the universal donor, because the antibody and antigen considerations are inverted.",
          "FALSE. Primary components are refused, but many fractions, cell salvage and recombinant products are matters of personal conscience — each patient's wishes must be documented individually and in advance."]},

    {cat:"Cardiopulmonary bypass", stem:"Regarding cardiopulmonary bypass:",
     opts:["A roller pump delivers pulsatile flow.",
           "A standard CPB circuit comprises venous reservoir, oxygenator, heat exchanger, arterial pump and arterial line filter.",
           "The target pump flow on full cardiopulmonary bypass in a normothermic adult is about 1.2 L/min/m².",
           "Centrifugal pumps are afterload-dependent, so flow falls if the arterial line is partially occluded.",
           "Membrane oxygenators have largely replaced bubble oxygenators because they cause less blood trauma."],
     ans:["FALSE. Roller pumps deliver essentially NON-pulsatile flow, as do centrifugal pumps. Loss of a pulse pressure is why pulse oximetry and non-invasive blood pressure fail on full bypass.",
          "TRUE — with a cardiotomy suction, cardioplegia delivery line and a vent. Blood drains by gravity from the right atrium and is returned to the ascending aorta.",
          "FALSE. Full flow is about 2.2 to 2.4 L/min/m², matching resting cardiac index. Flow can be reduced with hypothermia because metabolic demand falls.",
          "TRUE — unlike a roller pump, which continues to generate pressure against an obstruction and can rupture the line. Centrifugal pumps are therefore less likely to cause massive air embolism or line disruption.",
          "TRUE — less haemolysis, platelet activation and gaseous microembolism, since there is no direct blood-gas interface. Hollow-fibre polymethylpentene membranes are standard."]},

    {cat:"Cardiopulmonary bypass", stem:"Concerning cardiopulmonary bypass:",
     opts:["Heparin for CPB is given at approximately 300 to 400 units/kg, targeting an activated clotting time above 480 seconds.",
           "Alpha-stat acid-base management requires carbon dioxide to be added to the oxygenator gas flow.",
           "Heparin resistance may be caused by antithrombin III deficiency.",
           "Cardioplegia arrests the heart in systole.",
           "Moderate hypothermia to 32 to 34 °C reduces metabolic oxygen demand."],
     ans:["TRUE — checked before cannulation and at intervals on bypass. Failure to achieve the target raises the possibility of heparin resistance.",
          "FALSE. That is pH-STAT. Alpha-stat accepts the alkaline drift of hypothermia, keeping values normal when measured at 37 °C; pH-stat adds CO2 to hold pH at 7.4 at the actual temperature, which increases cerebral blood flow but also embolic load.",
          "TRUE — heparin works by potentiating antithrombin III, so deficiency (congenital, or acquired after prolonged heparin exposure) blunts the response. Treatment is antithrombin concentrate or fresh frozen plasma.",
          "FALSE. Arrest is in DIASTOLE — a relaxed, flaccid heart with minimal oxygen consumption. A contracted, arrested heart (stone heart) is a sign of ischaemic injury, not of successful cardioplegia.",
          "TRUE — oxygen consumption falls roughly by half for each 10 °C drop (the Q10 effect), which buys tolerance of low flow and of circulatory arrest."]},

    {cat:"Cardiopulmonary bypass", stem:"With respect to cardiopulmonary bypass:",
     opts:["Haemodilution on bypass offsets the rise in blood viscosity caused by hypothermia.",
           "Cardioplegia solutions arrest the heart by depolarising the myocardium with a high potassium concentration.",
           "Pulse oximetry and capnography remain reliable monitors during full cardiopulmonary bypass.",
           "Retrograde cardioplegia is delivered through the coronary sinus.",
           "Significant aortic regurgitation causes left ventricular distension during antegrade cardioplegia delivery."],
     ans:["TRUE — the crystalloid prime dilutes the haematocrit, improving microcirculatory flow at low temperature, at the cost of reduced oxygen-carrying capacity and dilution of clotting factors.",
          "TRUE — classically cold hyperkalaemic solution, delivered as crystalloid or blood cardioplegia, with del Nido and Custodiol as single-dose alternatives.",
          "FALSE. Neither works — there is no pulsatile peripheral flow and the lungs are not ventilated. Monitoring shifts to line pressures, ACT, arterial and venous temperatures, in-line oxygenator saturation and gases, and cerebral oximetry.",
          "TRUE — used when aortic regurgitation prevents antegrade root delivery, or where severe coronary stenosis would leave territories unprotected. Antegrade delivery is into the aortic root or directly into the coronary ostia.",
          "TRUE — the solution regurgitates into the ventricle instead of perfusing the coronaries, so the ventricle distends and is poorly protected. A vent and retrograde delivery are the answer."]},

    {cat:"Cardiopulmonary bypass", stem:"In relation to cardiopulmonary bypass:",
     opts:["Cardiopulmonary bypass provokes a systemic inflammatory response.",
           "Deep hypothermic circulatory arrest at around 18 °C permits roughly 30 to 40 minutes of arrest.",
           "Cerebral injury after bypass is attributed chiefly to embolism and hypoperfusion.",
           "Vacuum-assisted venous drainage reduces the risk of air entrainment into the circuit.",
           "Tranexamic acid reduces blood loss in cardiac surgery but is associated with seizures at high dose."],
     ans:["TRUE — contact activation of complement, kallikrein and neutrophils on the circuit surfaces, amplified by endotoxaemia and ischaemia-reperfusion. It underlies the post-bypass vasodilatation, oedema and coagulopathy.",
          "TRUE — used for aortic arch surgery, often with selective antegrade cerebral perfusion to extend the safe period. Beyond it, neurological injury rises steeply.",
          "TRUE — particulate emboli from aortic manipulation, gaseous emboli from the circuit and open chambers, plus low perfusion pressure. Epiaortic scanning and reduced aortic handling are mitigations.",
          "FALSE. It INCREASES it — negative pressure on the venous line can draw air in around the cannula or through an open suture line, and also increases haemolysis and gaseous microemboli.",
          "TRUE — the benefit on transfusion is well established; high-dose regimens carry a dose-related risk of postoperative seizures, so lower-dose regimens are now favoured."]},

    {cat:"Cardiopulmonary bypass", stem:"As regards cardiopulmonary bypass:",
     opts:["Retrograde autologous priming and cell salvage reduce transfusion requirement.",
           "Low perfusion pressure, prolonged bypass time and haemodilution are risk factors for postoperative acute kidney injury.",
           "Off-pump coronary surgery avoids the inflammatory response of bypass but has not shown a clear mortality benefit.",
           "Protamine can cause severe pulmonary hypertension and cardiovascular collapse.",
           "Ventilation should be recommenced only after separation from bypass is complete."],
     ans:["TRUE — along with a minimised circuit, biocompatible coatings and point-of-care viscoelastic testing to guide targeted haemostatic therapy.",
          "TRUE — and AKI after cardiac surgery independently predicts mortality. There is no proven pharmacological prophylaxis; dopamine and furosemide do not prevent it.",
          "TRUE — less transfusion and shorter ventilation in trials, offset by a higher rate of incomplete revascularisation and repeat intervention. It demands haemodynamic tolerance of cardiac displacement.",
          "TRUE — three recognised reactions: dose-related hypotension from rapid injection, true anaphylaxis, and catastrophic pulmonary vasoconstriction. Give it slowly, through a central or peripheral line, with the surgeon warned.",
          "FALSE. The lungs must be re-expanded and ventilation restarted BEFORE separation, as part of the weaning checklist — rewarming, rhythm, electrolytes, haematocrit, de-airing, ventilation, vasoactive drugs ready and the TOE looking at ventricular function."]},

    {cat:"IABP & ECMO", stem:"Regarding IABP & ECMO:",
     opts:["The intra-aortic balloon inflates at the onset of systole to augment forward flow.",
           "The intra-aortic balloon inflates during diastole and deflates just before systole.",
           "An intra-aortic balloon pump is a useful treatment for severe aortic regurgitation.",
           "Inflation of the intra-aortic balloon is timed to the dicrotic notch of the arterial waveform.",
           "An intra-aortic balloon pump increases coronary perfusion pressure and decreases left ventricular afterload."],
     ans:["FALSE. Inflating in systole would obstruct ejection and raise afterload dramatically. Inflation is in DIASTOLE; this is the single most important timing fact about the device.",
          "TRUE — counterpulsation. Diastolic inflation augments coronary and systemic perfusion; pre-systolic deflation reduces afterload and so reduces myocardial work.",
          "FALSE. It is CONTRAINDICATED — diastolic inflation worsens the regurgitant volume. Other contraindications are aortic dissection, aortic aneurysm or stent graft, and severe peripheral vascular disease.",
          "TRUE — the notch marks aortic valve closure and the start of diastole. Deflation is timed to just before the upstroke of the next systole.",
          "TRUE — so myocardial oxygen supply rises while demand falls, the one combination few other interventions achieve. Cardiac output rises modestly, typically by 0.5 to 1 L/min."]},

    {cat:"IABP & ECMO", stem:"Concerning IABP & ECMO:",
     opts:["The balloon tip should lie in the descending aorta just distal to the left subclavian artery and above the renal arteries.",
           "The IABP-SHOCK II trial showed a clear mortality benefit for balloon pump use in cardiogenic shock after myocardial infarction.",
           "The balloon pump can be triggered from the ECG R wave or from the arterial pressure waveform.",
           "Carbon dioxide is used to inflate the balloon because of its high solubility in blood.",
           "Helium is used to inflate the balloon because of its low density and rapid gas transfer."],
     ans:["TRUE — at about the level of the carina or the second to third intercostal space on a chest radiograph. Too high occludes the subclavian or risks cerebral embolism; too low threatens the renal and mesenteric vessels.",
          "FALSE. It showed NO reduction in 30-day or longer-term mortality, which is why the balloon pump was downgraded in the guidelines to selected indications rather than routine use in infarct-related shock.",
          "TRUE — and intrinsic or pacemaker rhythm can be used. Irregular rhythms, particularly fast atrial fibrillation, make timing unreliable and reduce augmentation.",
          "FALSE. Helium is used — its low density allows fast inflation and deflation at high heart rates. CO2 is too dense to shuttle quickly enough.",
          "TRUE — low density means rapid shuttling in and out, and if the balloon ruptures helium is relatively rapidly absorbed. Blood in the gas line signals rupture and demands immediate removal."]},

    {cat:"IABP & ECMO", stem:"With respect to IABP & ECMO:",
     opts:["Limb ischaemia, thrombocytopenia and haemolysis are recognised complications of balloon counterpulsation.",
           "Veno-venous ECMO supports gas exchange but provides no direct circulatory support.",
           "Veno-venous ECMO is the appropriate configuration for cardiogenic shock.",
           "Veno-arterial ECMO provides both respiratory and circulatory support.",
           "Peripheral veno-arterial ECMO increases left ventricular afterload and may cause ventricular distension."],
     ans:["TRUE — along with balloon rupture, aortic dissection, infection and renal or mesenteric ischaemia from malposition. Distal limb perfusion must be checked regularly.",
          "TRUE — blood is drained from and returned to the venous system, so the patient's own heart must maintain the circulation. It is the configuration for isolated severe respiratory failure.",
          "FALSE. VV ECMO cannot support the circulation — it returns blood to the venous side. Cardiogenic shock needs VENO-ARTERIAL support, or a ventricular assist device.",
          "TRUE — blood is returned to the arterial system, bypassing heart and lungs. It is used for cardiogenic shock, refractory arrest and failure to wean from bypass.",
          "TRUE — retrograde flow up the aorta opposes ejection. If the ventricle cannot open the aortic valve, stasis, thrombus and pulmonary oedema follow, and the ventricle must be vented or unloaded."]},

    {cat:"IABP & ECMO", stem:"In relation to IABP & ECMO:",
     opts:["Differential hypoxia in peripheral VA ECMO causes desaturation of the upper body while the lower body remains well oxygenated.",
           "The CESAR and EOLIA trials examined ECMO in severe acute respiratory failure.",
           "Bleeding is the commonest complication of ECMO.",
           "Recirculation in veno-venous ECMO is reduced by placing the drainage and return cannulae close together.",
           "High shear stress in the circuit causes an acquired von Willebrand syndrome."],
     ans:["TRUE — the harlequin or north-south syndrome: poorly oxygenated native output perfuses the aortic arch while the oxygenated return perfuses the lower body. Monitor the RIGHT radial artery and consider a return limb to the internal jugular vein.",
          "TRUE — CESAR supported referral to an ECMO centre; EOLIA missed its primary endpoint but with a high crossover rate, and later analyses support ECMO in the most severe patients.",
          "TRUE — from systemic anticoagulation, platelet consumption and acquired von Willebrand syndrome. Cannula-site, intracranial and gastrointestinal bleeding dominate, and intracranial haemorrhage carries the worst outcome.",
          "FALSE. Proximity INCREASES recirculation — freshly oxygenated blood is drained straight back into the circuit. The cannulae should be well separated, or a dual-lumen cannula with directed return used.",
          "TRUE — high-molecular-weight von Willebrand multimers are cleaved, impairing platelet adhesion. It helps explain bleeding despite apparently adequate anticoagulation control."]},

    {cat:"IABP & ECMO", stem:"As regards IABP & ECMO:",
     opts:["Anticoagulation during ECMO is usually with unfractionated heparin, monitored by ACT or anti-Xa.",
           "Ultra-protective 'lung rest' ventilation is used once a patient is established on ECMO for respiratory failure.",
           "Extracorporeal CPR can be used in refractory cardiac arrest with a witnessed arrest and short low-flow time.",
           "Cannula-site infection and lower limb ischaemia are specific hazards of femoral arterial ECMO cannulation.",
           "ECMO treats the underlying lung or cardiac injury."],
     ans:["TRUE — with bivalirudin or argatroban as alternatives in heparin-induced thrombocytopenia. Targets are lower than for cardiopulmonary bypass.",
          "TRUE — very low tidal volumes and driving pressures with moderate PEEP, since gas exchange is handled by the circuit. The aim is to stop ventilator-induced lung injury.",
          "TRUE — in selected patients in systems able to deliver it quickly, with reversible causes such as massive pulmonary embolism, hypothermia, poisoning or refractory ventricular arrhythmia.",
          "TRUE — a distal perfusion cannula to the superficial femoral artery reduces limb ischaemia, and the limb must be assessed clinically and by near-infrared spectroscopy or Doppler.",
          "FALSE. It is purely SUPPORTIVE — a bridge to recovery, to transplantation, to a durable device, or to a decision. It buys time for the lung or heart to recover or for definitive treatment, and nothing more."]},

    {cat:"Congenital heart disease", stem:"Regarding congenital heart disease:",
     opts:["A left-to-right shunt causes cyanosis from birth.",
           "Ventricular septal defect is the commonest congenital cardiac malformation.",
           "Tetralogy of Fallot includes an atrial septal defect.",
           "VSD, ASD, PDA and atrioventricular septal defect produce left-to-right shunting and pulmonary overcirculation.",
           "Eisenmenger syndrome is irreversible pulmonary hypertension with reversal of a previously left-to-right shunt."],
     ans:["FALSE. It causes pulmonary overcirculation and heart failure, not cyanosis. Cyanosis appears only if the shunt reverses — Eisenmenger physiology — after years of pulmonary hypertension.",
          "TRUE — around a third of lesions, and most small defects close spontaneously. Bicuspid aortic valve is commoner still as a structural variant but is not usually counted among congenital defects.",
          "FALSE. The septal defect is VENTRICULAR. An associated ASD makes it a pentalogy, but the classic tetrad is a VSD.",
          "TRUE — presenting with failure to thrive, tachypnoea and recurrent chest infection rather than cyanosis, and with a risk of progressive pulmonary vascular disease if untreated.",
          "TRUE — and closing the defect at that stage is contraindicated, since the defect has become the ventricle's escape route. Management is pulmonary vasodilator therapy and, ultimately, transplantation."]},

    {cat:"Congenital heart disease", stem:"Concerning congenital heart disease:",
     opts:["A fall in systemic vascular resistance worsens cyanosis in Eisenmenger syndrome.",
           "Dobutamine is an appropriate treatment for a hypercyanotic spell.",
           "Tetralogy of Fallot comprises ventricular septal defect, right ventricular outflow obstruction, right ventricular hypertrophy and an overriding aorta.",
           "Indomethacin is used to maintain patency of the ductus arteriosus.",
           "A hypercyanotic spell in tetralogy of Fallot is treated with oxygen, knee-to-chest positioning, fluid, phenylephrine and a beta blocker."],
     ans:["TRUE — it increases right-to-left shunting. Spinal anaesthesia, vasodilators and hypovolaemia are therefore dangerous, and an alpha agonist should be immediately available.",
          "FALSE. Inotropes WORSEN the spell by increasing dynamic right ventricular outflow obstruction. The correct drugs are a pure alpha agonist to raise systemic resistance and a beta blocker such as esmolol to relax the outflow tract.",
          "TRUE — the degree of outflow obstruction determines the degree of cyanosis, and its dynamic component is what makes spells possible.",
          "FALSE. Indomethacin and ibuprofen CLOSE the duct, by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis — that is their role in the preterm infant with a symptomatic PDA. Prostaglandin E1 keeps it open.",
          "TRUE — raise systemic resistance, restore preload and relax the dynamic outflow obstruction. Opioid or sedation breaks the cycle of crying and hyperventilation."]},

    {cat:"Congenital heart disease", stem:"With respect to congenital heart disease:",
     opts:["Prostaglandin E1 maintains ductal patency in duct-dependent lesions.",
           "Transposition of the great arteries presents with cyanosis and is initially managed with prostaglandin and balloon atrial septostomy.",
           "Positive pressure ventilation improves pulmonary blood flow in a Fontan circulation.",
           "Coarctation of the aorta causes upper limb hypertension with radio-femoral delay.",
           "Pulmonary blood flow in a completed Fontan circulation is passive and depends on preload and low pulmonary vascular resistance."],
     ans:["TRUE — buying time in transposition, critical coarctation, pulmonary atresia and interrupted arch. Apnoea, hypotension and pyrexia are its recognised side effects, so the airway must be watched.",
          "TRUE — survival depends on mixing. Definitive repair is the arterial switch operation in the neonatal period.",
          "FALSE. It REDUCES it — raised mean intrathoracic pressure impedes passive flow through the lungs. Spontaneous ventilation is favoured, and if ventilation is required, short inspiratory times, low PEEP and early extubation.",
          "TRUE — blood pressure should be measured in the RIGHT arm, since the left subclavian may arise at or beyond the coarctation. Rib notching and a dilated collateral circulation appear later.",
          "TRUE — there is no sub-pulmonary ventricle. Hypovolaemia, raised PVR, loss of sinus rhythm and raised intrathoracic pressure are all poorly tolerated."]},

    {cat:"Congenital heart disease", stem:"In relation to congenital heart disease:",
     opts:["Meticulous de-airing of intravenous lines and the use of air filters are essential in any patient with a right-to-left shunt.",
           "A right-to-left shunt speeds intravenous induction and slows inhalational induction.",
           "Children with cyanotic congenital heart disease are typically polycythaemic.",
           "A left-to-right shunt markedly slows intravenous induction.",
           "Prolonged preoperative fasting is particularly hazardous in cyanotic and Fontan patients."],
     ans:["TRUE — even a small volume of air can cross to the systemic circulation and cause paradoxical cerebral or coronary embolism. This includes patients with a patent foramen ovale under conditions that reverse the gradient.",
          "TRUE — drug bypasses the lungs and reaches the brain sooner, while alveolar agent uptake is diluted by shunted blood. Give intravenous agents slowly and expect gaseous induction to take longer.",
          "TRUE — secondary erythrocytosis from chronic hypoxaemia, with hyperviscosity, a bleeding tendency from platelet and factor abnormalities, and a risk of thrombosis if dehydrated.",
          "FALSE. A left-to-right shunt has little clinically important effect on either intravenous or inhalational induction. It is the RIGHT-to-left shunt that alters induction kinetics.",
          "TRUE — both depend on adequate preload and low viscosity. Clear fluids should be encouraged up to the allowed limit and intravenous fluids started early."]},

    {cat:"Congenital heart disease", stem:"As regards congenital heart disease:",
     opts:["Down syndrome is associated with atrioventricular septal defect, subglottic stenosis and atlanto-axial instability.",
           "22q11 deletion (DiGeorge) is associated with conotruncal cardiac anomalies and hypocalcaemia.",
           "Pulse oximetry screening of newborns detects critical congenital heart disease that examination alone may miss.",
           "Adults with congenital heart disease now outnumber children with it.",
           "A normal haemoglobin in a chronically cyanotic child is reassuring."],
     ans:["TRUE — plus obstructive sleep apnoea, a large tongue, and a tendency to bradycardia on inhalational induction. A smaller tracheal tube than predicted by age should be available.",
          "TRUE — with thymic aplasia and immunodeficiency requiring irradiated blood products, a difficult airway from palatal and facial anomalies, and a need to check ionised calcium.",
          "TRUE — pre- and post-ductal saturations, now a routine screen. Duct-dependent lesions may be clinically silent until the duct closes.",
          "TRUE — a consequence of successful paediatric surgery, so general anaesthetists increasingly meet repaired and palliated circulations. Late arrhythmia, ventricular failure and conduit or valve failure are the common problems.",
          "FALSE. It represents relative ANAEMIA — such a child should be polycythaemic, so a normal value suggests iron deficiency and reduced oxygen-carrying capacity. Iron deficiency also makes microcytes that worsen viscosity."]},

    {cat:"Procedural sedation", stem:"Regarding procedural sedation:",
     opts:["In deep sedation the patient still responds purposefully to verbal commands.",
           "Sedation is a continuum from minimal sedation through moderate and deep sedation to general anaesthesia.",
           "Pulse oximetry detects hypoventilation earlier than capnography.",
           "In moderate sedation the patient responds purposefully to verbal command or light touch.",
           "Sedation outside theatre requires the same standards of assessment, fasting, consent, monitoring and recovery as general anaesthesia."],
     ans:["FALSE. Deep sedation means response only to REPEATED or PAINFUL stimulation. Airway intervention may be required and ventilation may be inadequate, which is why it demands the same facilities and skills as general anaesthesia.",
          "TRUE — with no sharp boundaries, so the practitioner must be able to rescue a patient from one level DEEPER than the level intended.",
          "FALSE. Oximetry is a LATE monitor of ventilation — desaturation lags apnoea by minutes, and supplemental oxygen delays it further. Capnography detects the problem at once.",
          "TRUE — with a patent airway, adequate spontaneous ventilation and usually stable cardiovascular function. Reflex withdrawal from pain does not count as a purposeful response.",
          "TRUE — with trained assistance, suction, oxygen, a tipping trolley, resuscitation equipment and a staffed recovery area. Lowering any of these because the technique is 'only sedation' is how harm occurs."]},

    {cat:"Procedural sedation", stem:"Concerning procedural sedation:",
     opts:["Capnography is recommended during moderate and deep sedation.",
           "Midazolam provides useful analgesia for a painful procedure.",
           "Supplemental oxygen delays desaturation but conceals hypoventilation.",
           "Target-controlled infusion of propofol removes the risk of unintended progression to general anaesthesia.",
           "Midazolam should be titrated in small increments with a 2 to 3 minute wait for effect, and much lower doses in the elderly."],
     ans:["TRUE — it detects apnoea and airway obstruction before the saturation falls, especially where supplemental oxygen is being given and where the patient is draped or out of direct sight.",
          "FALSE. It has NO analgesic action — it gives anxiolysis, sedation and anterograde amnesia. A patient who seems to need more midazolam for pain actually needs analgesia or local anaesthesia.",
          "TRUE — the saturation can remain normal through profound hypercapnia. This is the single strongest argument for capnography rather than reliance on the oximeter.",
          "FALSE. The model predicts a concentration; it does not measure the patient's response, and individual sensitivity varies several-fold. TCI makes titration smoother but the depth must still be assessed clinically.",
          "TRUE — the commonest cause of oversedation is giving the next increment before the last has worked. Elderly and frail patients may need a fraction of the standard dose."]},

    {cat:"Procedural sedation", stem:"With respect to procedural sedation:",
     opts:["Combining a benzodiazepine with an opioid produces synergistic respiratory depression.",
           "Flumazenil has a shorter half-life than midazolam, so resedation can occur.",
           "Ketamine abolishes airway protective reflexes.",
           "Propofol sedation has a narrow margin between sedation and general anaesthesia and no antagonist.",
           "Ketamine produces dissociative sedation with relatively preserved airway tone and respiration."],
     ans:["TRUE — far greater than either alone, and the commonest drug pattern in sedation-related critical incidents. Reduce both doses and allow longer between increments.",
          "TRUE — the patient must be observed for at least 2 hours afterwards. It can precipitate seizures in chronic benzodiazepine users and in mixed overdose with tricyclics, so it is not a routine reversal agent.",
          "FALSE. Reflexes are relatively PRESERVED, which is a large part of its appeal in remote and prehospital settings — but preservation is not a guarantee, and laryngospasm and aspiration can still occur.",
          "TRUE — so it should be given by someone trained and dedicated to managing the airway and cardiovascular consequences, not by the operator.",
          "TRUE — with analgesia, nystagmus, hypersalivation and a rise in heart rate and blood pressure. Emergence phenomena are reduced by a quiet environment and a small benzodiazepine dose."]},

    {cat:"Procedural sedation", stem:"In relation to procedural sedation:",
     opts:["Dexmedetomidine produces sedation from which the patient can be roused, with little respiratory depression.",
           "Fifty per cent nitrous oxide in oxygen is contraindicated in pneumothorax, bowel obstruction and recent intraocular gas injection.",
           "Remimazolam is an ultra-short-acting benzodiazepine metabolised by tissue esterases and reversible with flumazenil.",
           "Sedation is a safer choice than general anaesthesia in a patient with a known difficult airway.",
           "Obstructive sleep apnoea, ASA III or IV status and poor cardiorespiratory reserve are indications for sedation to be given by an anaesthetist."],
     ans:["TRUE — an alpha-2 agonist, useful for awake fibreoptic intubation and awake craniotomy. Bradycardia and hypotension are the limiting effects, and onset is slow.",
          "TRUE — because it diffuses into air-filled spaces far faster than nitrogen leaves them. Decompression illness and recent middle-ear surgery are further contraindications.",
          "TRUE — giving organ-independent offset with the safety of an available antagonist, which is its attraction for procedural sedation in frail patients.",
          "FALSE. It is often MORE dangerous — an obstructed or apnoeic sedated patient in an awkward position with no secured airway is the worst situation to be in. A planned secured airway is usually the safer option.",
          "TRUE — along with a predicted difficult airway, a long or painful procedure, and extremes of age. Pre-sedation assessment exists to identify exactly these patients."]},

    {cat:"Procedural sedation", stem:"As regards procedural sedation:",
     opts:["Most serious morbidity from procedural sedation is respiratory in origin.",
           "Discharge after sedation requires documented criteria, a responsible adult escort and written instructions.",
           "Children have a higher rate of sedation failure and of adverse airway events than adults.",
           "Local or regional anaesthesia with good explanation may remove the need for sedation altogether.",
           "The person giving deep sedation may also perform the procedure."],
     ans:["TRUE — airway obstruction, apnoea and hypoventilation, compounded by inadequate monitoring and a failure to recognise and rescue. It is a systems problem more than a drug problem.",
          "TRUE — no driving, operating machinery, alcohol or important decisions for at least 24 hours, with a contact number. Scoring systems such as the modified post-anaesthetic discharge score formalise the assessment.",
          "TRUE — with the greatest risk in those under one year and in those with comorbidity. Paediatric sedation needs child-specific guidance, dosing and recovery facilities.",
          "TRUE — and is the safest option in frail patients. Sedation should complement adequate local anaesthesia, never substitute for it.",
          "FALSE. Deep sedation requires a dedicated, appropriately trained practitioner whose SOLE responsibility is the patient. The operator-sedationist model is restricted to minimal and conscious sedation in defined settings."]}

  ]
});
